1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,912
This is STAT 331, video 18. It is a review of the Midterm 1 question types.

2
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:29,954
After this video, you can place each Midterm 1 question in its type. You can write the two-sample problem as a regression, and derive its estimates. You can interpret the slope of a standardized x, and show why the p-value does not change. These are Tutorial 2, Question 2, and Assignment 1, Question 2, parts b and c.

3
00:00:30,404 --> 00:00:41,391
Every Midterm 1 question starts from the simple linear regression model. y i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 x i, plus epsilon i.

4
00:00:41,841 --> 00:00:59,078
Theory questions ask you to derive a mean, a variance, or a distribution. Or they ask which model assumption a result uses. Tutorial 1, Question 3, and Assignment 1, Question 1, are of this type. Earlier videos solve them.

5
00:00:59,528 --> 00:01:12,563
Applied questions give a coefficient table or data summaries. You calculate t values, p-values, confidence intervals, and prediction intervals. Earlier videos also solve these.

6
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:28,757
This video does one more theory type. The question changes the x variable. Then it asks what happens to the estimates and to the tests. Tutorial 2 codes x as 0 or 1. Assignment 1 standardizes x.

7
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:52,332
The two problems of this video use only four formulas from Lectures 2 and 3. First, the slope estimate. beta 1 hat equals S x y over S x x. S x x is the sum of x i minus x bar, squared. S x y is the sum of x i minus x bar, times y i minus y bar.

8
00:01:52,782 --> 00:02:07,609
Second, the intercept estimate. beta 0 hat equals y bar, minus beta 1 hat times x bar. So the fitted line goes through the point x bar, y bar. The purple lines show this point.

9
00:02:08,059 --> 00:02:20,795
Third, the standard error of beta 1 hat. It is sigma hat, over the square root of S x x. sigma hat squared is the sum of the squared residuals, over n minus 2.

10
00:02:21,245 --> 00:02:34,834
Fourth, the t statistic. It is beta 1 hat minus theta 0, over the standard error. Under the null hypothesis, it has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

11
00:02:35,284 --> 00:02:43,924
Tutorial 2, Question 2. The usual two-sample problem can be written as a regression problem. Pause the video and try it.

12
00:02:44,374 --> 00:02:59,286
The first n 1 subjects have x equal to 0. The other n 2 subjects have x equal to 1. So x only says which group a subject is in. In the picture, all points of a group are on one vertical line.

13
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:08,568
y bar 0 is the sample mean of group 0. y bar 1 is the sample mean of group 1. The purple marks show them.

14
00:03:09,018 --> 00:03:22,586
The pictures use our own small example. Group 0 has the values 3, 5, 4, and 6. Its mean is 4.5. Group 1 has 8, 7, and 9. Its mean is 8.

15
00:03:23,036 --> 00:03:36,668
Part a. Put x equal to 0 into the mean of the model. The mean response of group 0 is beta 0. Each group has a normal distribution around its mean, with variance sigma squared.

16
00:03:37,118 --> 00:03:43,219
Put x equal to 1. The mean response of group 1 is beta 0 plus beta 1.

17
00:03:43,669 --> 00:03:59,157
Subtract the two means. beta 1 is the difference in mean response between group 1 and group 0. This is the slope interpretation of Lecture 2, with a step of one unit from x equal 0 to x equal 1.

18
00:03:59,607 --> 00:04:15,885
So the two groups have the same mean exactly when beta 1 is 0. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals 0. The alternative is beta 1 is not 0. The red curve shows group 1 under the null hypothesis.

19
00:04:16,335 --> 00:04:33,743
Part b. Pause the video and try it. The plan: find x bar, S x x, and S x y. Then use the two formulas for beta 1 hat and beta 0 hat. The picture shows the x values: n 1 zeros and n 2 ones.

20
00:04:34,193 --> 00:04:48,507
x bar is the sum of the x i, over n. The zeros add nothing. Each one adds 1. So the sum is n 2, and x bar is n 2 over n. In the example, x bar is 3 over 7.

21
00:04:48,957 --> 00:05:05,811
Now the deviations from x bar. For group 0, x i minus x bar is minus n 2 over n. For group 1, it is 1 minus n 2 over n. This is n 1 over n, because n minus n 2 is n 1.

22
00:05:06,261 --> 00:05:30,709
S x x adds the squared deviations. n 1 squares of n 2 over n, and n 2 squares of n 1 over n. Take out n 1 n 2 over n squared. What is left is n 2 plus n 1, which is n. So S x x is n 1 times n 2, over n. In the example, 4 times 3 over 7, which is 12 over 7.

23
00:05:31,159 --> 00:05:44,748
Next, S x y. The sum of x i minus x bar, times y bar, is y bar times 0. So S x y is the sum of x i minus x bar, times y i.

24
00:05:45,198 --> 00:05:57,166
Each y value gets a weight: its x deviation. Every value in group 0 gets minus n 2 over n. Every value in group 1 gets n 1 over n.

25
00:05:57,616 --> 00:06:17,349
The sum of the y values in group 0 is n 1 times y bar 0. The sum in group 1 is n 2 times y bar 1. So S x y is minus n 2 over n, times n 1 y bar 0, plus n 1 over n, times n 2 y bar 1.

26
00:06:17,799 --> 00:06:41,565
Take out n 1 n 2 over n. S x y equals n 1 n 2 over n, times y bar 1 minus y bar 0. In the example: minus 3 over 7 times 18, plus 4 over 7 times 24. That is 6. And the formula gives 12 over 7, times 3.5, which is also 6.

27
00:06:42,015 --> 00:06:58,121
Divide S x y by S x x. The factor n 1 n 2 over n cancels. So beta 1 hat is y bar 1 minus y bar 0. In the example, 8 minus 4.5, which is 3.5.

28
00:06:58,571 --> 00:07:14,806
For beta 0 hat we need y bar, the mean of all n values. It is n 1 y bar 0 plus n 2 y bar 1, over n. In the example, it is 6. The purple point x bar, y bar is on the line.

29
00:07:15,256 --> 00:07:35,181
Now beta 0 hat is y bar, minus beta 1 hat times n 2 over n. Put everything over n. The n 2 y bar 1 terms cancel. What is left is n 1 plus n 2, times y bar 0, over n. So beta 0 hat is y bar 0.

30
00:07:35,631 --> 00:07:47,237
This is the result. beta 0 hat is the mean of group 0. beta 1 hat is the difference of the two group means. The fitted line goes through both group means.

31
00:07:47,687 --> 00:08:11,559
Here is a second way to show part b. Write the least squares criterion S. For group 0, x i is 0, so each term is y i minus beta 0, squared. For group 1, x i is 1, so each term is y i minus beta 0 minus beta 1, squared. The gray line is one candidate line, with its residuals.

32
00:08:12,009 --> 00:08:25,299
Call beta 0 plus beta 1 by the name m. Then the first sum uses only beta 0, and the second sum uses only m. So we can make each sum a minimum on its own.

33
00:08:25,749 --> 00:08:45,888
Take the derivative of the first sum with respect to beta 0, and set it to 0. The sum of y i minus beta 0 is 0, so beta 0 is the mean of group 0. In the same way, m hat is the mean of group 1. Now the residuals are the distances to the group means.

34
00:08:46,338 --> 00:08:57,474
Then beta 1 hat is m hat minus beta 0 hat: y bar 1 minus y bar 0. This is the same answer. Use either way on the exam.

35
00:08:57,924 --> 00:09:19,108
Now use the example to test the null hypothesis, beta 1 equals 0. The fitted values are the group means. So each residual is y i minus its group mean. The squared residuals add to 5 plus 2, which is 7. Divide by n minus 2, which is 5. sigma hat squared is 1.4.

36
00:09:19,558 --> 00:09:47,633
The standard error of beta 1 hat is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. The square root of 1.4, over the square root of 12 over 7, is 0.9037. The red tails show the rejection region at the 5 percent level. The critical value t 0.975 with 5 degrees of freedom is 2.571. On the exam, this quantile is given.

37
00:09:48,083 --> 00:10:11,677
The t statistic is 3.5 over 0.9037, which is 3.873. It is larger than 2.571. The two-sided p-value is 0.0117. So we reject the null hypothesis at the 5 percent level. The data give evidence that the two group means are different.

38
00:10:12,127 --> 00:10:35,935
This part is optional, and not on the slides. 1 over S x x is 1 over n 1, plus 1 over n 2. So the standard error is sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n 1 plus 1 over n 2. And sigma hat squared is the pooled variance of the two groups. So this regression test is the pooled two-sample t test.

39
00:10:36,385 --> 00:10:57,676
Assignment 1, Question 2, parts b and c. Model A is the usual model in x. Model C uses a new variable, x star. x star is x minus x bar, over s x. Part b asks for the meaning of beta 1 C. Part c compares the two p-values. Pause the video and try it.

40
00:10:58,126 --> 00:11:08,174
The pictures use the brainhead data. x is head size in cubic centimeters. The x star ruler measures the same head sizes in a new unit.

41
00:11:08,624 --> 00:11:19,227
At x equal to x bar, 3637.9, x star is 0. So x star is 0 at the centre of the data.

42
00:11:19,677 --> 00:11:36,999
One step on the x star ruler is s x, which is 361.1 cubic centimeters. s x is the square root of S x x over n minus 1. So one unit of x star is one standard deviation of x.

43
00:11:37,449 --> 00:11:51,295
Solve the definition of x star for x. x i equals x bar, plus s x times x i star. The picture shows the mean line of the brainhead data, with the x axis from 0.

44
00:11:51,745 --> 00:12:05,078
Put this into the mean of Model A. In Model A, the intercept beta 0 is the mean of y at x equal to 0. For head size, x equal to 0 is far outside the data.

45
00:12:05,528 --> 00:12:20,312
Multiply out and group the terms. The constant term is beta 0 plus beta 1 x bar. This is the mean of y at x equal to x bar, the purple point. It is the intercept of Model C.

46
00:12:20,762 --> 00:12:38,383
The coefficient of x i star is s x times beta 1. This is the slope of Model C. It is the rise of the line over a run of one standard deviation. So both models describe the same mean line. Only the x scale changes.

47
00:12:38,833 --> 00:12:45,553
Part b. Here is the brainhead scatter plot. The x axis is head size in cubic centimeters.

48
00:12:46,003 --> 00:12:54,707
Under it is the x star axis. Its 0 is at x bar. Each step of 1 is one standard deviation of head size.

49
00:12:55,157 --> 00:12:58,379
The fitted line is the same line in both scales.

50
00:12:58,829 --> 00:13:24,386
beta 1 C is the difference in mean brain weight between two populations whose head sizes differ by one sample standard deviation, s x. For these data, beta 1 C hat is 361.1 times 0.2608, which is 94.19 grams. beta 0 C is the mean of y at x equal to x bar.

51
00:13:24,836 --> 00:13:42,991
Now fit Model C with the least squares formulas. The x star values have mean 0. Their S x x is S x x of x, over s x squared. This is n minus 1, because s x squared is S x x over n minus 1.

52
00:13:43,441 --> 00:14:00,422
Each x star deviation is the x deviation over s x. So S x star y is S x y over s x. Divide. beta 1 C hat is s x times beta 1 hat. The estimates obey the same rule as the parameters.

53
00:14:00,872 --> 00:14:12,093
beta 0 C hat is y bar, minus beta 1 C hat times the mean of x star. That mean is 0. So beta 0 C hat is y bar.

54
00:14:12,543 --> 00:14:31,786
Now the fitted values. y bar plus beta 1 hat times x i minus x bar is the Model A fitted value. So the two models have the same fitted values and the same residuals. The orange segments are equal in both plots. So sigma hat is also the same.

55
00:14:32,236 --> 00:14:43,009
Part c. The test of beta 1 equals 0 in Model A uses t A: beta 1 hat, over sigma hat over the square root of S x x.

56
00:14:43,459 --> 00:15:02,489
In Model C, sigma hat is the same, and S x x is n minus 1. So the standard error is sigma hat over the square root of n minus 1. Since n minus 1 is S x x over s x squared, this is s x times the standard error of Model A.

57
00:15:02,939 --> 00:15:12,539
So t C is s x beta 1 hat, over s x times the standard error. The factor s x cancels. t C equals t A.

58
00:15:12,989 --> 00:15:35,133
Both statistics have a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom under the null hypothesis. The same value and the same distribution give the same p-value. So the two p-values are equal. Also, beta 1 C is 0 exactly when beta 1 is 0, so the two null hypotheses say the same thing.

59
00:15:35,583 --> 00:15:49,407
Check this with the brainhead data. Here is the Model A table from Lecture 3, page 45. The residual standard error is 72.35 on 234 degrees of freedom.

60
00:15:49,857 --> 00:16:17,099
We fit Model C to the same data. The slope estimate is 94.19, which is 361.1 times 0.26082. The standard error is 4.720, which is 361.1 times 0.01307. The t value is 19.957 in both tables. So the p-values are equal.

61
00:16:17,549 --> 00:16:36,152
The intercept rows are different. Model A tests whether the mean at x equal 0 is 0. Model C tests whether the mean at x bar is 0. These are different hypotheses, at different points on the line. So do not compare intercept tests between the two models.

62
00:16:36,602 --> 00:16:51,194
The confidence intervals agree too. The 95 percent interval for beta 1 C is s x times the interval for beta 1. Both intervals exclude 0. This agrees with the equal p-values.

63
00:16:51,644 --> 00:17:02,396
The same steps work for any linear change of x. This slide is a summary, and not on the slides. The blue ruler is head size in cubic centimeters.

64
00:17:02,846 --> 00:17:19,145
Units of 100 cubic centimeters: x prime is x over 100. The slope is multiplied by 100: 26.08 grams per 100 cubic centimeters. The t value does not change.

65
00:17:19,595 --> 00:17:31,541
The standardized x star from the assignment is x minus x bar, over s x. The slope is multiplied by s x. The t value again does not change.

66
00:17:31,991 --> 00:17:57,677
In general, for x prime equal to x minus a, over b, the slope and its standard error are multiplied by b. The t statistic keeps its size, so the two-sided p-value is the same. If b is negative, t changes sign. For example, swap the group codes 0 and 1 in Tutorial 2: the slope changes sign. The new intercept is the mean of y at x equal to a.

67
00:17:58,127 --> 00:18:14,361
To recap. With two groups coded 0 and 1, beta 0 is the mean of group 0, and beta 1 is the difference of the group means. The least squares estimates are y bar 0 and y bar 1 minus y bar 0.

68
00:18:14,811 --> 00:18:22,619
A test of no difference in means is the test of beta 1 equals 0, with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

69
00:18:23,069 --> 00:18:35,805
With a standardized x, beta 1 C is s x times beta 1: the mean difference per one standard deviation of x. beta 0 C is the mean of y at x bar.

70
00:18:36,255 --> 00:18:52,383
The fitted values and sigma hat do not change. The slope and its standard error are both multiplied by s x. So the t statistic and the p-value of the slope test are the same. This is the end of the Midterm 1 review.
